from acceptance; evals lines 103–131 distinguish assessment from promotion. These boundaries support coherent final canvases.","The collection avoids forcing every subsystem into the application runtime. static_analysis seed lines 89–90 explicitly identify a CLI integration boundary, retained in static_analysis/central line 60. composition lines 123–129 also explicitly define import arrows as permission relationships."],"issues":[{"evidence":"composition seed lines 80–111 include route families, a 64 MiB body limit, HTTP status mappings, and disposal order alongside application wiring. evals lines 66–93 enumerate workflow strategies and fixed few-shot counts; rendering lines 61–80 include measurement rounding and routing-cache conditions. The resulting composition/central is still 99 lines and evals/central 96 lines, before their peripherals.","impact":"Architectural boundaries compete with operational particulars at similar visual weight. The seeds favor comprehensive coverage of the supplied implementation over a clear reading priority, so later compression preserves large inventories. Exact defaults are sometimes useful, but their prominence can overfit the presentation to incidental implementation choices. Peripheral expansion and final dimensions remain downstream decisions.","recommendation":"Regenerate each seed around the behavior or boundary that makes its point distinctive. Keep exact limits, defaults, and implementation steps when they explain a consequential constraint; otherwise compress them beneath a meaningful contract. In composition, emphasize construction, shared instances, transport entry, and ownership. In evals, emphasize pinned evidence and transitions between preparation, execution, assessment, and promotion, with workflow-specific detail selected by explanatory value."},{"evidence":"diagrams seed lines 84–100 describe generated-edit validation and scoped replacement, overlapping diagram_generation lines 33–61. canvas_commands lines 118–121 describe candidate-view publication, overlapping chat lines 78–101. Final diagrams/generated and diagram_generation/gate both expand submission mechanics; canvas_commands/views lines 13–20 and chat/isolate lines 22–25 both explain candidate publication. Within parsing, seed lines 17–26 become closely repeated material in central lines 13–18 and folding lines 2–9.","impact":"The collection repeatedly restarts explanations of shared mechanisms. Some repetition provides necessary local context, but the seeds do not consistently distinguish a point’s owned explanation from a short dependency contract. The later stage adds its own central/peripheral duplication, amplifying this ambiguity rather than originating all of it.","recommendation":"Assign shared mechanisms a clear explanatory home while retaining brief, locally useful summaries elsewhere. Let diagrams own revision and continuity semantics, diagram_generation own orchestration and installation, canvas_commands own saved-view behavior, and chat own candidate conversation coordination. Make secondary mentions state what is consumed or invoked and where the deeper explanation belongs. Do not remove repetition that explains a genuinely different consequence."},{"evidence":"An end-to-end publication chain is distributed across rendering seed line 34 ('controller.commitEditorGesture'), canvas_commands line 18 ('historyService.append -> publish controller snapshot'), workspace lines 33 and 55–72 (persistence notification and delivery), and persistence lines 38–52 (blob/manifest publication). chat explicitly references [agent_access], [contexts], and [canvas_commands] at lines 39, 79, and 88, but comparable handoffs elsewhere are usually implicit. Final rendering and canvas_commands edges predominantly connect central concepts to explanatory peripherals; workspace additionally connects session to records and records to delivery.","impact":"Individual trees explain their components, but readers must reconstruct how the collection’s components cooperate. A tree branch may organize a topic, list owned state, or describe execution; those meanings are often carried only by local wording. Most final edges then express elaboration rather than the system relationships spanning points. Actual cross-canvas navigation is a later responsibility, but clearer handoffs could have been supplied by Phase 2.","recommendation":"Name important producer/consumer handoffs in the seeds using consistent component names and explicit verbs. For the publication chain, identify the semantic commit, emitted checkpoint changes, delivery acknowledgement, and manifest visibility boundary at their respective owners. Use cross-point references where they resolve a real dependency. Preserve hierarchical grouping without implying that sibling order or indentation alone establishes execution or ownership."},{"evidence":"Several distinct identity scopes are locally described but not explicitly connected: diagrams seed lines 4–7 distinguish diagramId, revisionId, local Diagram.id, and parentId; line 17 constructs content with 'id:revision', while parsing line 22 calls the content-node prefix 'revisionId'. canvas_commands line 11 checks snapshot.version, workspace line 61 sends expectedRevision, persistence line 64 checks expectedRevisionId, and changes lines 15 and 23 discuss document and session versions. Final diagrams/identity lines 16–18 adds the important distinction between numeric content revision and immutable revision identity.","impact":"Repeated terms such as revision, version, and ID can appear interchangeable across otherwise detailed points. Readers can follow an individual check yet struggle to understand which identity survives an edit, which version guards a mutation, and which revision belongs to publication. This is a presentation ambiguity established by the labels, not an assertion that the underlying implementation confuses them.","recommendation":"Clarify identity scope at the relevant handoffs: local content key, logical diagram identity, immutable revision identity, parser/content identity, document version, session version, and storage/catalog revision where applicable. Explain conversions or bindings when they matter, using the existing owning points rather than repeating a universal glossary in every tree."},{"evidence":"agent_access seed line 23 says 'Mutations require current expectedVersion', whereas final agent_access/save lines 21–25 makes requirements operation-specific and identifies create/copy exceptions. changes seed lines 22–24 broadly groups version checks; final changes/edits lines 11–14 separates document-version, session-version, and unversioned creation. chat seed lines 116–118 describes disposal as interrupting chats and persisting/flushing; final chat/lifetime lines 16–20 instead distinguishes marking state, queued saves, and resources not removed there. composition seed line 122 says dependency direction is 'enforced', while final composition/rules lines 22–25 limits the evidence to test rules without execution results.","impact":"Broad seed wording turns local checks, cleanup calls, or intended rules into apparent guarantees. The final outputs spend substantial space narrowing these claims. Their reported corrections are output evidence, not independent source verification, but they demonstrate that the seed formulations did not preserve scope reliably. Qualification added later can also leave a stronger central summary beside a narrower peripheral explanation.","recommendation":"Regenerate guarantees with their operation and boundary attached. Distinguish invoking cleanup from completing it, queued persistence from acknowledged persistence, and a test’s policy from demonstrated enforcement. State version requirements for the relevant operation families instead of generalizing across all mutations. Carry uncertainty or delegated behavior compactly where it affects interpretation; avoid expanding every limitation into a separate audit narrative."}],"regenerationInstructions":"Regenerate the 15 seeds as a coordinated collection with distinct explanatory responsibilities. Preserve the successful separations among content, placement, live interaction, durable publication, pinned evidence, proposals, and evaluation. Reduce incidental mechanics where they obscure those boundaries; retain detail that explains a meaningful consequence or exception. Give shared mechanisms an explanatory home, keep concise dependency summaries elsewhere, and make important cross-point handoffs explicit. Clarify identity and version scopes and qualify guarantees at their actual boundary. Use grouping and relationship wording suited to each point, without a fixed template or branch count. Treat final-output refinements as evidence of presentation ambiguity to investigate, not as independently verified source facts. Final layout, peripheral counts, added implementation detail, and navigation remain later-phase responsibilities."}{"ascii":"Repository Evidence and Navigation
|-- Resolve an explicit diagram source [application/core/source/diagramSource.ts]
|   |-- Load project -> require project.id == reference.projectId
|   |-- Choose diagram
|   |   |-- Live diagram -> validate; check contentId when supplied
|   |   |-- Saved representation
|   |   |   |-- Requested revision or currentRevisionId must belong to representation
|   |   |   `-- Revision -> scene -> exactly one matching diagram
|   |   |       `-- Filter by contentId, or by presence of a source binding
|   |   `-- No representation or live diagram
|   |       |-- Merge catalog bindings + project.sources; optionally filter sourceId
|   |       |-- Same sourceId with different root, kind or repositoryIdentity -> error
|   |       `-- Require exactly one binding -> synthetic source-query diagram
|   |-- Supplied sourceId must match the chosen diagram's binding
|   `-- diagramSource(diagram) -> query + diagram + binding() + readText/listFiles
|       |-- Missing binding -> source-repository error
|       `-- File access uses binding.corpus [shared A]; never assumes one project repository
|
|-- Selected diagram nodes -> mapped evidence [domain/mapping/code-panel/codePanel.ts]
|   |-- handleNodeSelect: sort selected nodes by line, then column
|   |-- Build source-range index
|   |   |-- sourceContext.contentHash must equal content.asciiSha256
|   |   |   `-- Mismatch -> reject stale diagram mappings
|   |   |-- diagramSource.entries() joins mapping, entity, captured range and binding
|   |   |   `-- Resolve content node ID; skip entries without node, entity or range
|   |   `-- Resource target
|   |       |-- repo: projectId + entity.sourceId, else binding.sourceId, else empty ID
|   |       |-- path + inclusive, one-based startLine/endLine
|   |       `-- rangeSha256 = SHA-256(captured originalLines joined with LF)
|   |-- resolveNodeResources(all content nodes, selected IDs, index)
|   |   |-- Matches -> resources with selected-node and mapped-node provenance
|   |   `-- Unmapped selections -> unmappedNodeIds
|   |-- Deduplicate by type + project/source + path + bounds + range hash
|   |   `-- Combine origins: selected ID/label, mapped ID/label, mappingId
|   `-- PanelContent -> diagram/revision/hash + selections + unmapped IDs + resources
|       `-- CodePanel [src/features/mapping/code-panel/CodePanel.tsx]
